{"status":"productive","notes":"Both Bundestag PDF text and the bifg factsheet text were unreadable via WebFetch. I got the factsheet by reading the saved PDF with the Read tool, which showed the chart as an image. The BARMER figures are a ~10% population sample and the data stop at Q1 2025. A quarterly numeric series exists only as a chart in the factsheet (Abb. 1) and in Table 2 of the Gynäkologische Praxis paper, which I did not open. I did not open the Drucksache body text, only its hib summary (29 Jan 2026) and the PDF metadata. Net read: uptake jumped from near 0 in H2 2022 to ~480/1,000 pregnancies by Q1 2024 and has plateaued since, with only slow upward drift in 2024-25. That is saturation in Germany's NIPT volume, not a growth driver. The risk side is political: the Bundestag motion asks for monitoring results by end-June 2027 and an expert panel. This is a very small test in Gb terms (~1-2 Gb per NIPT), so the Illumina read-through is weak.","how_to_mine":"Open https://www.bifg.de/media/dl/Journals/gynaekologische_praxis_2026.pdf and read Table 2 (quarterly service frequencies) and the age-specific figures. Use a PDF reader that can render text, since WebFetch returns only metadata. Also pull the interactive ePaper or Versorgungskompass 'Geburtshilfe und Hebammenversorgung Teil 4' at bifg.de for numeric quarterly values by age. Cross-check with G-BA or KBV billing counts of EBM code for NIPT (GOP 01789) per year for a national denominator (~680K births). Open the Drucksache 21/3873 body to confirm the exact asks and any vote or committee schedule.","difficulty":"easy","items":[{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"German statutory NIPT uptake (BARMER) jumped after coverage began in Jul 2022, then plateaued by early 2024, so this volume stream is saturating.","detail":"The bifg/BARMER factsheet (data Jan 2022 to Q1 2025, ~10% population sample, denominator pregnancies by birth date) shows near-zero uptake until H2 2022, then a steep climb through 2